The heap window: every low_4gb mapping lies in one window at a base aligned to 4 GiB (ADR-0033 decision 3).

ART keeps a reference to an object as its address in 32 bits and asks MemMap for its heap with low_4gb, which
on arm64 scans [64 KiB, 4 GiB). iOS maps nothing below 4 GiB. At its first low_4gb request, MemMap now reserves a
window of 4 GiB without access at a base aligned to 4 GiB, so that the low 32 bits of an address in it are its
offset. Every low_4gb mapping takes a range of the window: at the address it asks for, where that lies in the
window and is free, as mmap treats a hint, else at the top of the highest gap that holds it. So the window fills
from the top, and every object has an offset above 2^31 from the start, where a reference sign-extended somewhere
shows at once. The window's first page stays without access for good, so no object has the offset 0, which is
null, and so does its last page, so no mapping ends at 2^32, whose 32 bits are 0 as well. What MemMap unmaps in the
window goes back to the reservation. Where a mapping goes and which ranges are mapped is base/heap_window_ranges.h,
a file of the overlay, which runtime/tests/heap_window_test.cpp checks on the Mac.

